    <section class="empire-glance" id="at-a-glance" aria-labelledby="glance-title">
      <div class="empire-glance-main"><h2 class="empire-glance-title" id="glance-title">At a glance</h2><ul><li>A kingdom of western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mexico">Mexico</a> centred on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lake-patzcuaro">Lake Pátzcuaro</a>, ruled from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tzintzuntzan">Tzintzuntzan</a> by a king the Spaniards called the cazonci.</li><li>In the late 1470s it routed an Aztec army led by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/axayacatl">Axayacatl</a>, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aztec-empire">Aztecs</a> never conquered it.</li><li>Its smiths led <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mesoamerica">Mesoamerica</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/copper">copper</a> and bronze; it submitted to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a> in 1522, and its last king was executed in 1530.</li></ul></div>
      <aside class="empire-dyk"><p class="empire-dyk-title">Did you know?</p><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/relacion-de-michoacan">Relación de Michoacán</a>, the main source for the kingdom’s history, was illustrated with 44 paintings by Purépecha artists and is kept today in the royal library of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/el-escorial">El Escorial</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a>.</p></aside>
    </section>

