    <div class="profile-section-block" id="foundation">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Foundation</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/antonio-de-mendoza"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/antonio-de-mendoza.jpg" alt="Antonio de Mendoza" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/antonio-de-mendoza">Antonio de Mendoza</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:AntonioMendoza.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Unknown author, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The Purépecha Empire, called the Tarascan state by the Spaniards, grew up around <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lake-patzcuaro">Lake Pátzcuaro</a> in the volcanic highlands of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/michoacan">Michoacán</a>, in western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mexico">Mexico</a>. Its early history is known chiefly from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/relacion-de-michoacan">Relación de Michoacán</a>, compiled around 1539–1541, probably by the Franciscan friar Jerónimo de Alcalá, for Viceroy <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/antonio-de-mendoza">Antonio de Mendoza</a> from the testimony of Purépecha nobles and priests. In this account a lineage called the uacúsecha, ‘eagles’, of Chichimec origin, entered the region near Zacapu and moved to the shores of the lake, where they contended with the other lords of the basin. In the fourteenth century their leader Tariácuri, lord of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/patzcuaro">Pátzcuaro</a>, brought the lake communities under his control through war and alliance. He divided his realm among three heirs, his son Hiquíngare at Pátzcuaro and his nephews Hiripan at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ihuatzio">Ihuatzio</a> and Tangáxoan at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tzintzuntzan">Tzintzuntzan</a>, who carried the conquests beyond the basin. Archaeology broadly agrees with this outline: the state took shape in the Pátzcuaro basin in the fourteenth century, when the population of the basin was growing rapidly, and its rulers fostered a new state religion and ideology to support their authority. By the middle of the fifteenth century power was concentrated at Tzintzuntzan, which remained the capital until the Spanish conquest.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="socioeconomy">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Socioeconomy</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/curicaueri"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/curicaueri.jpg" alt="Curicaueri" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/curicaueri">Curicaueri</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Exvoto_para_Curicaueri.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Josuemirandazepeda, CC BY-SA 4.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>On the eve of the Spanish conquest the kingdom covered some 75,000 square kilometres in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/michoacan">Michoacán</a> and parts of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guanajuato">Guanajuato</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jalisco">Jalisco</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guerrero">Guerrero</a>, with perhaps more than a million inhabitants. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lake-patzcuaro">Lake Pátzcuaro</a> basin alone held some 80,000 people in about 90 settlements, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tzintzuntzan">Tzintzuntzan</a>, with perhaps 25,000 to 35,000, was the kingdom’s only true city. Besides the Purépecha, whose language is unrelated to any other in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mesoamerica">Mesoamerica</a>, the kingdom included <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nahuas">Nahua</a>, Otomí, Matlatzinca and other peoples, who kept their own languages but owed tribute and, in war, warriors. Lake fishing, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maize">maize</a> farming and the resources of mountains, forests and hot lowlands sustained the economy, and tribute brought <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cotton">cotton</a> cloth, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cacao">cacao</a>, salt and feathers to the royal storehouses. The Purépecha were the foremost metalworkers of Mesoamerica. Using techniques that had reached western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mexico">Mexico</a> from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/south-america">South America</a> centuries earlier, their smiths worked <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/copper">copper</a> from the La Huacana area, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silver">silver</a> from Tamazula and gold from placers in Michoacán and Colima, alloying copper with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tin">tin</a> or arsenic to make bronze for bells, tweezers, ornaments and tools. Obsidian, worked into blades, earspools and lip plugs, was another prized resource. Religion centred on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/curicaueri">Curicaueri</a>, a god of fire and the sun and patron of the royal lineage, and on goddesses such as Cuerauáperi, honoured at Zinapécuaro.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="military">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Military</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/axayacatl"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/axayacatl.jpg" alt="Axayacatl" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/axayacatl">Axayacatl</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Axayacatl.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The kingdom was built by conquest. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/relacion-de-michoacan">Relación de Michoacán</a> describes the campaigns of Tariácuri and his heirs, and by the late fifteenth century Purépecha armies had carried the frontier north to Acámbaro, near the Lerma River, west into <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jalisco">Jalisco</a> and south into the Balsas basin. From about 1460 this expansion met that of the Aztec <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aztec-empire">Triple Alliance</a>, and the two powers fought along a long frontier until the Spanish conquest. In the late 1470s (sources date the campaign between 1476 and 1479) the Aztec ruler <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/axayacatl">Axayacatl</a> led a large army into <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/michoacan">Michoacán</a> and was routed near Taximaroa, now Ciudad Hidalgo; the chronicler Diego Durán recorded that some 20,000 of his men died. It is often described as the heaviest defeat the Aztecs suffered before the Spanish arrived. Both sides then fortified the border. The Aztecs built a great fortress at Oztuma, in the Balsas region, while the Purépecha held strongholds such as Acámbaro, Taximaroa and Cutzamala and settled Otomí and Matlatzinca groups on the border as frontier guards; a report of 1580 credits Cutzamala with a garrison of 10,000 warriors. Under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ahuitzotl">Ahuitzotl</a> (r. 1486–1502) the Aztecs renewed the pressure through allied peoples of the Balsas region, but King Zuangua held the frontier, and the Aztecs never conquered the kingdom.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="governance">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Governance &amp; Law</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The Purépecha kingdom may have been the most centralised state of Postclassic <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mesoamerica">Mesoamerica</a>. At its head stood the king, called irecha in Purépecha and cazonci by the Spaniards, from a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nahuatl">Nahuatl</a> word, who ruled as the earthly representative of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/curicaueri">Curicaueri</a>; kingship remained in the lineage of Tariácuri. Beneath him a hierarchy of royal kin, high nobles and lesser nobles held the offices of war, government and the royal household, while the high priest, the petámuti, headed a priesthood whose members wore tobacco gourds as a sign of office. Conquered towns were governed through local lords answerable to the king, and a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tributary-system">tributary system</a>, said to have been organised under Hiripan and Tangáxoan, channelled cloth, food, metals and labour to the capital. The state also controlled mines, forests, fisheries and many craft workshops. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tzintzuntzan">Tzintzuntzan</a> was a royal and administrative city. On a great platform some 450 by 250 metres above the lake stood five keyhole-shaped temple platforms, the yácatas, each once crowned by a wooden temple, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/relacion-de-michoacan">Relación de Michoacán</a> mentions palaces, storehouses for grain and tribute, a prison, a zoo, baths, a ballcourt and a market. Royal funerals were great ceremonies at which servants were killed to accompany the dead king.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="impact">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Global &amp; Regional Impact</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/vasco-de-quiroga"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/vasco-de-quiroga.jpg" alt="Vasco de Quiroga" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/vasco-de-quiroga">Vasco de Quiroga</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Vasco_de_Quiroga.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Original uploader was Rbraunwa at en.wikipedia, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>For more than half a century the Purépecha kingdom was the strongest of the states that resisted the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aztec-empire">Aztec Empire</a>, and the Aztec advance into western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mexico">Mexico</a> stopped at its frontier, a heavily militarised border lined with fortresses on both sides. Its metallurgy, the most advanced in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mesoamerica">Mesoamerica</a>, gave <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/copper">copper</a> and bronze a place in ritual, warfare and daily life, and copperwork remains a craft of the region. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/relacion-de-michoacan">Relación de Michoacán</a>, illustrated with 44 paintings by Purépecha artists and now kept in the royal library of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/el-escorial">El Escorial</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a>, is one of the richest indigenous histories of colonial Mexico and the main source for the kingdom’s past. After the conquest <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vasco-de-quiroga">Vasco de Quiroga</a>, sent to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/michoacan">Michoacán</a> as a royal inspector in 1533 and appointed its first <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">bishop</a> in 1536, founded hospital-towns for the Purépecha and encouraged villages to specialise in particular crafts, a pattern still visible in the guitars of Paracho, the pottery of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tzintzuntzan">Tzintzuntzan</a> and the copper of Santa Clara del Cobre; he is still remembered in the region as ‘Tata Vasco’. The Purépecha people endure: some 140,000 people spoke their language in the 2020 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/census">census</a>, and the yácatas of Tzintzuntzan and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ihuatzio">Ihuatzio</a> are protected archaeological sites.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="decline">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Decline &amp; Fall</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/tangaxuan-ii"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/tangaxuan-ii.jpg" alt="Tangaxuan II" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/tangaxuan-ii">Tangaxuan II</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Tangaxuan_II_being_executed.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Juan O'Gorman, CC BY 2.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The kingdom fell to epidemic disease and Spanish coercion rather than to defeat in battle. When <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mexica">Mexica</a> envoys came to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tzintzuntzan">Tzintzuntzan</a> seeking an alliance against the Spaniards, King Zuangua sent no army, and in 1520 he died, probably in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/smallpox">smallpox</a> epidemic that had spread from the Spanish expedition. His son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tangaxuan-ii">Tangaxuan II</a>, also called Tzintzicha, succeeded him; according to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/relacion-de-michoacan">Relación de Michoacán</a> he had the members of a later Mexica embassy sacrificed so that they could carry their message to his dead father. After the fall of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tenochtitlan">Tenochtitlan</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hernan-cortes">Hernán Cortés</a> sent Cristóbal de Olid into <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/michoacan">Michoacán</a>, and in the summer of 1522 Olid entered Tzintzuntzan without a battle, while the Spaniards gathered gold and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silver">silver</a> from its temples and royal tombs. Tangaxuan II submitted and for some years kept much of his authority, his subjects paying tribute both to him and to the Spaniards. In 1529–1530 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nuno-de-guzman">Nuño de Guzmán</a>, president of the First <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/audiencia">Audiencia</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/viceroyalty-of-new-spain">New Spain</a>, on his way to conquer western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mexico">Mexico</a>, arrested the king with the help of the Purépecha noble Pedro Cuiniarángari, tortured him in search of gold and tried him for rebellion, withholding tribute and other charges. On 14 February 1530 he was executed; the sentence ordered him garrotted and burned, and his ashes were thrown into the Lerma River.</p></div>
      </div>

    <p class="profile-summary" id="summary"><em>The Purépecha Empire, ruled from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tzintzuntzan">Tzintzuntzan</a> on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lake-patzcuaro">Lake Pátzcuaro</a>, was the second largest state of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mesoamerica">Mesoamerica</a> on the eve of the Spanish conquest; it routed an Aztec army in the late 1470s, was never conquered by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aztec-empire">Aztecs</a>, and was the leading centre of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/copper">copper</a> and bronze metallurgy in the region.</em></p>

